  "description": "SUPERSEDED on the same host by deepseek-v4-1-flash-fp8-rtxpro6000-vllm-tp4 (2026-09-18). As measured here (2048-token prefill chunks, 4.2M-token KV pool) this SGLang configuration dies with CUDA OOM in the low-ratio indexer on any cold prefill deeper than about 265k to 290k tokens despite its 524,288 context limit: the indexer materialises about three [chunk x context] float32 score buffers per chunk and only about 6.5 GiB per GPU is left for activations. It survives with 1024-token chunks and a 3.8M pool at 409,600 context (cold prefill about 25 to 30 percent slower). Kept as a record of the measurements and of the fixes: bounded 40 GiB host Engram cache with parallel NVMe misses (row_store v2), HiCache sized to host RAM, prefill-decode interval 1. Local Studio controller recipe for native-precision DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash on four RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell cards with 125 GiB of host RAM: SGLang TP4/EP4 with B12X SM120 kernels, DSpark block-5 speculative decoding, vision, 524,288-token context and a 4.2M-token KV pool. FP4 Engram tables stay on NVMe behind a bounded 40 GiB host row cache; cache misses are read in parallel (row_store v2, 32 I/O threads, packed scales), which raised cold prefill from about 1,000 to 7,200-7,900 tok/s. HiCache ratio 1.0 pins about 29 GB of host KV, and the boot script clamps the Engram cache so pinned plus cached host memory cannot exceed RAM (ratio 1.5 with a 64 GiB cache crashed the server hours after boot). --prefill-decode-interval 1 keeps running requests generating during long prefills. The serving image is a local unpublished build, so this stays a candidate.",
